>
  <p>
    One of the most common questions people have about influenza is: <strong>"How long am I contagious?"</strong>
    The answer is more nuanced than most people realize — and knowing the timeline can help you protect
    your family, coworkers, and community.
  </p>

  <div class="callout-info">
    <strong>Quick answer:</strong> Adults with the flu are most contagious <strong>1 day before symptoms appear 
    until 5-7 days after becoming sick</strong>. Children and people with weakened immune systems 
    may be contagious for <strong>longer than 7 days</strong>.
  </div>

  <h2>The Flu Contagious Timeline</h2>

  <h3>Before Symptoms: Already Contagious</h3>
  <p>
    Most people don't realize this, but <strong>you can spread the flu before you even know you're sick</strong>.
    According to the CDC, people with influenza are contagious starting about <strong>1 day before symptoms develop</strong> [1].
    This is one reason the flu spreads so efficiently — people go about their normal activities
    while unknowingly infecting others.
  </p>

  <h3>Day 1-3 of Symptoms: Peak Contagiousness</h3>
  <p>
    The <strong>first 3-4 days of illness</strong> are when you're most contagious [2]. During this period:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Viral shedding is at its highest level</li>
    <li>Fever is typically present, which correlates with higher transmission risk</li>
    <li>Coughing and sneezing actively spread virus particles</li>
    <li>You should <strong>absolutely stay home</strong> during this period</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Day 5-7: Contagiousness Declines</h3>
  <p>
    After about 5 days, viral shedding decreases significantly in most healthy adults.
    The general rule: you can return to normal activities when:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>You've been <strong>fever-free for at least 24 hours</strong> (without using fever-reducing medication)</li>
    <li>Your other symptoms are improving</li>
    <li>It's been at least <strong>5 days since symptoms began</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Special Cases: Who's Contagious Longer?</h2>

  <h3>Children</h3>
  <p>
    Children — especially those under 5 — can shed influenza virus for <strong>10 days or longer</strong> [3].
    This is why schools and daycares are such efficient transmission environments.
    Children should stay home until they meet the same criteria as adults
    (24 hours fever-free + improving symptoms).
  </p>

  <h3>Immunocompromised Individuals</h3>
  <p>
    People with weakened immune systems — including those undergoing chemotherapy, transplant recipients,
    or those with HIV/AIDS — may remain contagious for <strong>weeks or even months</strong> [1].
    These individuals should consult their healthcare provider for personalized guidance
    on isolation and return to normal activities.
  </p>

  <h3>Older Adults (65+)</h3>
  <p>
    Older adults may shed the virus for slightly longer than younger adults —
    typically up to <strong>7-10 days</strong>. Combined with their higher risk of complications,
    extra caution is warranted.
  </p>

  <h2>How Is the Flu Spread?</h2>
  <p>Understanding transmission helps you protect others during the contagious period:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Respiratory droplets:</strong> Coughing, sneezing, or talking within 6 feet</li>
    <li><strong>Surface contact:</strong> Virus can survive on surfaces for up to 24-48 hours [2]</li>
    <li><strong>Before symptoms:</strong> 1 day prior to feeling sick</li>
    <li><strong>After fever resolves:</strong> Up to 5-7 more days of potential transmission</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When Can I Return to Work or School?</h2>
  <p>
    The CDC recommends staying home for at least <strong>4-5 days after symptoms begin</strong>
    and until you've been <strong>fever-free for 24 hours without medication</strong> [1].
    Even after returning, practice good hygiene:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Wear a mask in crowded spaces for a few more days</li>
    <li>Continue frequent hand washing</li>
    <li>Cover coughs and sneezes</li>
    <li>Avoid close contact with high-risk individuals</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout-warning">
    <strong>Important:</strong> If you work in healthcare, childcare, or with vulnerable populations,
    your workplace may have stricter return-to-work policies. Follow your employer's guidelines.
  </div>

  <h2>How to Protect Others While Contagious</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Stay home</strong> — especially during the first 4-5 days</li>
    <li><strong>Isolate within your home</strong> — use a separate bedroom and bathroom if possible</li>
    <li><strong>Wear a mask</strong> if you must be around others</li>
    <li><strong>Wash hands frequently</strong> with soap and water for 20+ seconds</li>
    <li><strong>Disinfect surfaces</strong> — doorknobs, light switches, phones, remotes</li>
    <li><strong>Cover coughs and sneezes</strong> with a tissue or your elbow</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid close contact</strong> with high-risk individuals for the full 7-10 days</li>
  </ol>

